[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Service Discovery & Registration in Distributed Systems"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a modern microservices architecture, services scale dynamically. Instances are constantly spinning up, shutting down, or moving across different hosts due to auto-scaling and failures. Hardcoding IP addresses and ports is no longer feasible. Service Discovery and Registration solve this problem by providing an automated mechanism for services to locate and communicate with each other."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Service Registry: A centralized database that contains the network locations of all service instances."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Service Registration: The process by which a service instance adds its network address to the registry upon startup."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Service Discovery: The process by which a client or router queries the registry to find an available instance of a target service."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Discovery"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are two primary patterns for implementing service discovery: Client-Side and Server-Side."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Client-Side Discovery: The client is responsible for querying the service registry, selecting an available instance using a load-balancing algorithm (like Round Robin), and making the request directly."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Server-Side Discovery: The client makes a request to a load balancer or router. The router queries the service registry and forwards the request to an available service instance."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Consul Registration Config"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example JSON configuration for registering a microservice with HashiCorp Consul using a health check."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"{
  "ID": "order-service-1",
  "Name": "order-service",
  "Address": "192.168.1.10",
  "Port": 8080,
  "Check": {
    "HTTP": "http://192.168.1.10:8080/health",
    "Interval": "10s"
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement Health Checks: Ensure the registry actively removes dead instances by using TCP, HTTP, or TTL-based health checks."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Caching: Clients or API gateways should cache service locations locally to reduce load on the registry and prevent cascading failures."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Favor CP over AP for Registries: Consistent and Partition-tolerant systems (like Consul or Zookeeper) are generally preferred for service registries over Available systems to avoid split-brain states."}]
